     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered the order. The FDA label doesn't support the premise for this dose.\n\n- The label's maximum for immediate-release metformin is 2,550 mg/day, given as 850 mg three times daily.\n- For extended-release metformin, the maximum is 2,000 mg/day.\n- 1,700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day, which is twice the label maximum. No label formulation or indication allows it.\n\nIf you're looking at a different source, tell me which one and I'll look at it with you. Entering a 2\u00d7 overdose as written isn't something I can do on that basis.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum).\n- Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals (2,000 mg/day).\n- Metformin 1,700 mg PO per day, split as 850 mg twice daily with meals.\n\nIf you need a dose above the label maximum for a specific reason, that decision should go through pharmacy and the attending. They can enter or override it through the hospital's high-dose process."
      }
